Finding this measurement
was the hardest part.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.500
Now that they have it,
they were able to build

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.350
an entire system purely based off of this.

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:11.183
Let me show you.

00:10:11.183 --> 00:10:13.470
Okay, so now we want a unit for volume.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.210
Let's build a hypothetical cube

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:18.090
that is 10 centimeters on all sides.

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:21.720
Fill it up with liquid,
and you have a liter.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:22.590
One liter.

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:23.460
And from there, you can do

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:25.110
the same thing that we did with meters.

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:28.020
Centiliters, kiloliters, you name it.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.390
It's all tens, which
makes it so much easier

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:32.940
to convert between these
different measurements.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:35.460
It's a lot easier than barley corns.

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:37.680
Okay, so now we have a liter,
which is a volume thing.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:42.421
Let's use this to make a
unit for mass, AKA weight.

00:10:42.421 --> 00:10:44.460
(keyboard typing)

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:45.930
I know they're different, but it's like,

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.090
the earth's gravity makes
weight and mass the same

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:49.830
when you're on Earth, blah, blah, blah.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.061
I get it, commenters. I see you.

00:10:52.061 --> 00:10:53.640
I see you typing right now. Back off.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:57.240
You fill this liter with water,
and now you have a kilogram.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.210
Divide that by 1000, and you have a gram.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:03.540
Add 1000 kilograms together,
and you have a metric ton.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:04.500
I mean, it's simple.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:06.690
It's tens, it's hundreds, it's thousands.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:08.670
- [Announcer] Now, many
calculations are merely

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:12.393
a matter of moving the
decimal point, not the pencil.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:15.540
- Okay, so now they have all these units

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:17.340
that are scientific and standardized

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:20.610
and founded on concepts
of exact measurements.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:23.430
And this is really the first
time that humans ever had this.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:24.847
The Frenchmen all looked at
each other and were like,

00:11:24.847 --> 00:11:27.277
"Good work, guys, we did
it. The coffee helped.

00:11:27.277 --> 00:11:29.700
"This is great. What should we call it?"

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:31.145
And they settled on...

00:11:31.145 --> 00:11:34.506
♪ The metric system ♪

00:11:34.506 --> 00:11:36.000
♪ The metric system ♪

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.290
- Okay, cool, great system.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.210
But why don't I use the metric system?

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:40.203
What happened?

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:42.780
Well, that has to do with

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:45.630
pirates and international politics.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.970
So France is like, this
system works pretty damn well.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:48.960
We're impressed with ourselves.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.480
We should tell the rest
of the world posthaste.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.650
So it's the late 1700's,

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:54.870
and France going around country by country

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.587
with a literal meter stick, being like,

00:11:56.587 --> 00:11:57.667
"Hey guys, look what we invented.

00:11:57.667 --> 00:11:58.567
"It's pegged to the earth.

00:11:58.567 --> 00:12:00.693
"It's literally like we
measured this entire thing,

00:12:00.693 --> 00:12:03.427
"and you should adopt it
because it's really precise.

00:12:03.427 --> 00:12:06.240
"It's gonna make international
trade way more efficient."

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:07.073
And people were like,

00:12:07.073 --> 00:12:09.450
"oh, this is cool, but
we're a little skeptical."

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.970
And meanwhile, in France, they
were still kind of skeptical.

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:14.640
The current leader at the
time was this guy, Napoleon.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:17.340
We've all heard about him.
And he was super into it.

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:18.420
He's like, "metric is awesome."

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:19.950
You know what else he was into?

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:21.750
Making sure everyone liked him.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:24.300
So when he saw that the French
public wasn't super jazzed

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.460
about uprooting what was working for them

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:27.930
for hundreds of years,

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:30.810
he decided not to enforce metric by law.

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:34.020
So France ended up in this
weird limbo for thirty years,

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:36.810
and meanwhile the US is
looking from across the ocean,

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.667
and they're like, "dude,
metric, it's a total fad.

00:12:39.667 --> 00:12:42.967
"There's no way. Napoleon is wishy-washy.

00:12:42.967 --> 00:12:45.067
"There's no way this is
actually gonna stick.

00:12:45.067 --> 00:12:47.017
"Let's hold off on the whole metric thing

00:12:47.017 --> 00:12:50.407
"and just industrialize
with our very silly system

00:12:50.407 --> 00:12:52.950
"that is built on, like, people's feet."

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:56.400
- The metric system is
the tool of the devil!

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:00.000
My car gets 40 rods to the hog's head,

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.250
and that's the way I likes it!

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:03.960
- But we'll get back
to the US in a minute,

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:06.180
because there's still hope.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:08.940
In the meantime, France
finally gets fully on board,

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:11.790
and successfully sells
not only their public

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.830
on the metric system, but the world.

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:15.067
They're like, "come on, guys.

00:13:15.067 --> 00:13:17.707
"We sent two dudes out to do trigonometry

00:13:17.707 --> 00:13:19.237
"with their golden telescopes

00:13:19.237 --> 00:13:21.217
"for like seven years for this shit!

00:13:21.217 --> 00:13:23.677
"Trust me, you don't want
to do that on your own.

00:13:23.677 --> 00:13:25.260
"Adopt the system."

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.270
It was a tough sell at first
to get people to switch,

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:28.880
but eventually, they did.

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:31.080
It started with Belgium,
then the Netherlands,

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.240
then Luxembourg, then Algeria,

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:35.280
a French colony, 20 years later.

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:37.110
And then it kind of snowballed from there,

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:38.850
partly because countries
realized how rational it was,

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:40.380
but partly because Europe was

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:41.700
taking over the world at this time,

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:43.980
and bringing their values and language

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:47.643
and systems to every corner
of the globe forcibly.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:49.613
Ah, yes.

00:13:49.613 --> 00:13:52.110
Colonialism will make it
into every one of my videos.

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:54.030
Okay, but what about the USA?

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:57.180
Well, France had some beef
with the US at this point.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:58.860
It's kind of a long story.
Not gonna go into it.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:00.630
But what's important
to know is that the US

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:03.120
was weirdly put in the
middle between this, like,

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:06.990
frenemy bromance gone wrong
between France and England.

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:09.000
So at first, America wasn't invited

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:11.430
to the inner circle to
be introduced to metric,

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:13.080
but as the world was adopting metric,

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:17.850
France finally decided to send
a guy over to deliver this:

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:22.230
a prototype of the almighty,
ultra-logical kilogram.

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:24.660
Inviting the US to join
the rest of the world

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:26.550
in this measurement revolution.

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:29.073
There is hope for the USA to go metric.

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:32.490
So this French ship is
sailing across the Atlantic

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.140
with the kilogram onboard

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:37.440
and a storm hits it and
blows it into the Caribbean,

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:41.460
where it runs into a
group of British pirates.

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:45.180
The British pirates capture
the emissary from France,

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.110
they imprison him,

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.840
and they try to gain ransom
from France for this guy.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:51.420
Well, they end up accidentally
killing the French guy,

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:53.460
but they notice that he has a package,

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:56.077
and the pirates are like, "Oh,
shit, this is addressed to

00:14:56.077 --> 00:14:58.747
"the US Secretary of
State Thomas Jefferson.

00:14:58.747 --> 00:15:00.607
"Us Brits are cool with the US right now,

00:15:00.607 --> 00:15:03.360
"so let's bring this over
to New York and deliver it."

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:06.150
Super curious pirates. I like these guys.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:07.380
But when they get to New York,

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:10.080
Thomas Jefferson isn't the
Secretary of State anymore.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:11.280
So they just hand it to the guy

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:13.830
who is now the Secretary
of State, Edmund Randolph.

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:14.707
And Randolph is like,

00:15:14.707 --> 00:15:16.950
"what the hell is this
weird hunk of copper?"

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:19.923
So then he gives it to this
other guy, who just keeps it.

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:23.730
So, yeah, thanks to
geopolitics and pirates,

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:25.560
the US didn't get the kilo in time

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:27.270
to convince them to maybe adopt

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:29.850
France's new super-logical
system of measurement.

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:31.860
Britain also refused for a long time,

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:33.210
sticking with the system that was

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:34.800
developed using some dude's foot,

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:36.420
but eventually, they got on board,

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:37.380
but not fully.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.140
They still dabble in
some old Imperial ways.

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:43.380
But get this: they've changed
some of the Imperial things

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:45.960
since the US became
independent, like their pint,

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:49.020
which is like 25% bigger
than the pint here.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:50.160
They're both called the pint,

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:51.810
but go get a pint of beer in the UK,

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:53.880
and you're actually getting 4 ounces more

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:54.900
than you would in the US.

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.660
As if having one highly illogical,

00:15:57.660 --> 00:15:59.310
unreliable measuring system

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:01.710
including feet and
teaspoons isn't bad enough,

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:03.240
there are now two of them floating around

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:04.710
that have the same names!

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:07.320
It gets real bad when
you look at the ounce,

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:09.840
which is a word that is used to define

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:14.460
several different units of
mass, weight, or volume.

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:17.043
Same word, bunch of different things.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:20.040
Okay, okay, but how hard can this be?

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:23.710
This is my home turf. This
is my measurement system.

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:25.920
(Pencil sharpening)

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:27.150
I got this.

00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:30.720
So an ounce in the UK is
one-twentieth an Imperial pint.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:32.580
That seems nice and easy.

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:36.360
That's one 160th of an
Imperial gallon, approximately.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:40.860
There's approximately 28.41 milliliters.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:42.000
Kind of gross.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:45.270
Okay, that's the Imperial
fluid ounce used in the UK.

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:46.350
Okay, so then the US ounce

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:49.110
is one-sixteenth of a US liquid pint,

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:51.120
which is one 128th a liquid gallon,

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:55.890
which is exactly
29.5735295625 milliliters,

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:58.530
making it about 4.08% larger

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:00.660
than the Imperial fluid
ounce used in the UK.

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:02.490
Okay, but wait. This is
just the fluid ounce.

00:17:02.490 --> 00:17:03.630
What about the weight ounce?

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:04.463
The one that came from, like,

00:17:04.463 --> 00:17:06.613
the seven thousand grains to be a pound.

00:17:06.613 --> 00:17:08.190
Okay, so seven thousand grains to a pound,

00:17:08.190 --> 00:17:09.810
and there's sixteen ounces to that pound,

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:13.200
so that's 437.5 grains to an ounce.

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:14.070
But if I want to convert

00:17:14.070 --> 00:17:15.540
the weight ounce to the fluid ounce,

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:17.083
I just need to multiply the volume by

00:17:17.083 --> 00:17:22.080
1.043176 times the density
of the ingredient or material

00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:24.360
so that we can easily switch between

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:26.520
the fluid ounce and the weight ounce,

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:29.850
and the pound with the 7000
grains, and sixteenth...

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:32.420
There's, one-sixteenth
of a pound is the ounce.

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:35.220
So grains are 437.5...

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:38.850
Just convert it with
Google, and you've got it.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:39.693
Just super easy.

00:17:42.031 --> 00:17:42.864
(sighs)

00:17:42.864 --> 00:17:45.270
Yeah, that's where we're
at. It's a total mess.

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:46.284
- [Interviewer] Do you know

00:17:46.284 --> 00:17:47.700
how many feet there are in a mile?

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:49.260
- Three thousand something.

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:50.670
- Every country on earth

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:52.530
adopted the metric system eventually

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:55.140
besides these three countries:

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:56.760
the US, Myanmar, and Liberia.

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.100
- The United States just
gave the rest of the world

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.770
a big finger and said,
"(beep) your metric system!"

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:03.990
- Over the years, the US has attempted

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:06.030
really formally to go metric.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:08.400
Like, in the 70's and 80's,
there were all these debates

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:10.170
and there were boards
created by the government

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:12.780
to find a way to migrate over to metric.

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:14.550
But, of course, Ronald
Reagan shut it down,

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:17.490
because of, you know, the whole
government spending thing.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.140
This was an actual public debate.

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:22.770
Like, this documentary I
keep showing was from a PSA

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:25.320
trying to convince people
to get on board with metric.

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:26.760
And then on the other side, in the 80's,

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:28.410
there was this anti-metric group.

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:30.960
Yes, anti-metric groups. That was a thing.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.570
They even hosted a gala,
where they featured

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:36.240
the Most Beautiful Foot contest.

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:37.880
Don't you just love American culture?

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:39.570
We just love to stick it to people.

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:42.630
Just look at us, being
individuals in our own ways.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:45.690
But this stuff actually matters, okay?

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:48.330
In 1999, NASA sent a satellite to Mars,

00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:49.530
and it went off-course

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:51.510
and burned up in the planet's atmosphere

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:55.500
all because of a misconversion
between units of measure.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:57.630
One of the contractors
was using imperial units

00:18:57.630 --> 00:18:59.820
while NASA Ground was using metric,

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:03.150
and it ended up costing
NASA 125 million dollars.

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:05.280
But what's interesting is
that the US has kind of

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:08.070
secretly quietly got on board with metric,

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:09.390
but it's just sort of behind the scenes

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.640
in boring sectors that none
of us really think about

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:12.630
and that don't actually make it

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:14.400
into all of our intuitions.

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:16.290
It's like industry, or
the federal government.

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:17.880
Or, like, nutrition labels.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:20.970
I don't look at nutrition
labels, but if I did,

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:22.690
I would see grams

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:25.023
and milligrams.

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:28.680
And on liquid-based things,
I would see milliliters,

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:30.960
because nutrition labels are in metric.

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:32.250
It doesn't matter that I don't really know

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:35.160
what a gram or a kilogram
or a milligram even is,

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:36.060
but when I go to the pharmacy,

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:38.430
the pharmacist gives me
my medicine in milligrams.

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:40.593
Progress. (chews)

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:43.260
So, yeah, it's pharmaceuticals,

00:19:43.260 --> 00:19:45.630
it's nutrition, it's film, it's tools,

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:48.300
it's bicycles, it's running races.

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:52.230
Oh, and for some reason, this
one highway sign in Arizona.

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:55.080
For all of these efforts,
none of them have succeeded

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:57.240
in getting metric into the brains

00:19:57.240 --> 00:19:59.007
of everyday people like me.

00:19:59.007 --> 00:20:03.240
And this is why I will
never use the metric system.

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:05.940
I can't! I literally cannot.

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:09.210
You don't realize how your
intuition is calibrated

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:11.040
in certain systems of measurements

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:12.450
until you try to change it.

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:14.670
I started traveling in my early 20's,

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:16.590
really for the first time abroad.

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:18.240
And I saw them using metric,

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:20.700
and I saw how easy it was
and how rational it was.

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:21.900
And I made a commitment to myself.

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:25.410
I'm like, I'm gonna change
from my imperial intuition

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:26.640
to adopting metric.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:28.710
And I even swore, I'm going to teach

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:31.440
my future children
metric from an early age.

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.010
And since then, over a
decade, I have been trying.

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:36.450
I look at this distance, and to me,

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:38.670
oh, that's ten meters
away, not thirty feet.

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:41.430
It feels like it's 20 degrees
Celsius outside right now,

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:42.263
I would say.

00:20:42.263 --> 00:20:44.880
Oh, yeah, it's like five
kilometers down the road.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.770
Oh, I'm gonna pick up
two kilos of short rib

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:47.940
to make the soup tonight.

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:49.530
But my dirty little secret is that

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:51.840
I've totally been faking it.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:54.270
Ten meters doesn't mean anything to me.

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:58.170
What it means is thirty
feet divided by three.

00:20:58.170 --> 00:20:59.730
What I've really been
doing this whole time

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:02.910
is intuitively estimating things
based on the system I know,

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:06.030
which is pegged to farming
activities and body parts,

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:08.280
and then doing the rough math

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:11.520
based on the numbers I know
about converting to metric.

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:13.650
Without those conversion numbers,

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:17.340
I honestly cannot estimate
what fifty meters looks like.

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:20.430
I can't grasp what running
five kilometers feels like.

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:23.070
I don't know what
twenty-two centimeters is

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:26.490
outside of the context
of a one-foot-long ruler

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:28.620
that I know is thirty centimeters.

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.540
And so I'll never use the metric system.

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:33.690
I don't know how, and
I can't teach myself.

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:34.523
I've tried.

00:21:34.523 --> 00:21:35.730
But then I had kids!

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:37.500
There's hope. The next generation.

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:40.260
I vowed to instill them
with metric intuition

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:42.990
and found that it's
basically been impossible

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:45.120
because everything they're exposed to

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:48.570
at school, in math, on road
trips while we're driving,

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.610
all of it is in feet and miles

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:54.750
and cups and pints and teaspoons and tons.

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:55.680
I mean, I try.

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:58.290
I tell my kids measurements in meters,

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:00.427
and my six-year-old will
literally respond with,

00:22:00.427 --> 00:22:02.160
"Well, how far is that in feet?"

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:05.400
He is six. Like, this isn't happening.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:08.490
The ship has sailed on changing
to metric here in the US

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:09.450
for everyday people.

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:10.800
It'll likely never happen.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:13.590
I'm trapped in a system that was developed

00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:17.850
using people's body parts
and farming activities

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:20.280
and King Henry's arm.

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:21.300
- [Announcer] We interrupt this film

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:23.954
to bring you a special
bulletin from Metric News.

00:22:23.954 --> 00:22:25.710
(jaunty big band music)

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:27.870
As America moves toward the metric system,

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:31.352
the last holdouts have been
seen finally switching over.

00:22:31.352 --> 00:22:34.080
Soon, it is predicted, the
whole world will be unified

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:36.633
under this easier system of measurement.

